    "ls|LS17_S8_U_250": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF EDUCATION \nDEPARTMENT OF SCHOOL EDUCATION & LITERACY \n \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 250 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21.03.2022 \n \n \nPM Poshan Yojana \n \n†250. SHRI SATYADEV PACHAURI:  \n  \nWill the Minister of EDUCATION be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether the Government has renamed Mid Day Meal Scheme as Pradhan Mantri Poshan \nShakti Nirman Scheme; \n \n(b)  if so, the additional difference and benefits likely to accrue in the said scheme with the \nchange of the name thereof; \n \n(c) whether there is any proposal to cover more number of children in addition to the 12 crore \nchildren of 11 lakh government schools under the said scheme to address the problem of \nmalnutrition; and \n \n(d)  if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF EDUCATION \n (SHRI DHARMENDRA PRADHAN) \n \n \n(a)  to (d): A statement is laid on the table of the House. \n  \n\n \nStatement referred to in reply to parts (a) to (d ) of Lok Sabha Starred Question No. 250 \nfor 21.03.2022  raised by Shri Satyadev Pachauri , Hon’ble MP  regarding PM Poshan  \nYojana. \n \n(a)  to (d): Government has approved the Centrally Sponsored Scheme ‘Pradhan Mantri \nPoshan Shakti Nirman (PM POSHAN)’  earlier known as ‘National Programme for Mid -\nDay Meal in Schools’ for providing hot cooked meal to eligible children in Government and \nGovernment – aided Schools from 2021 -22 to 2025-26. The Government has also approved \nfollowing additional features under PM POSHAN Scheme in order to address the problem of \nmalnutrition and improve the nutritional status of eligible children: \n \n(i) Inclusion of Balvatika: The provision of hot cooked meal to children of pre -schools \nor Balvatika (before class I) in primary schools also, in addition to the 11.80 crore \nchildren of classes I to VIII studying in 11.20 lakh Government and Governm ent-\naided schools. \n \n(ii) Tithi Bhojan : Tithi Bhojan is a community participation programme in which \npeople provide special food to children on special occasions/festivals in addition to \nregular meal.  \n \n(iii) School Nutrition Gardens : Under the Scheme, development of School Nutrition \nGardens in schools is being promoted,  to give children first hand experience with \nnature and gardening.      \n \n(iv) Social Audit : Social Audit in all districts is made mandatory under the Scheme. \nSocial Audit is collective monitoring of a scheme by people’s active involvement \ncovering issues of equity, equality and expenditure management.  \n \n(v) Vocal for local: Under ‘Vocal for local’ States and Union Territory Administrations \nare encouraged to decide menu suitable to the local conditions within the prescribed \nnutrition and food norms and to procure locally grown food items like millets, \nvegetables, condiments etc. from Farmers’ Produce rs Organizations, Federation of \nWomen’s Self-Help Groups etc. so as to promote local employment generation and \neconomic development while simultaneously improving the nutrition standards.  \n  \n(vi) Special Focus : Adequate provision for supplementary nutrition in aspirational \ndistricts / tribal districts / districts with identified high cases of malnutrition etc. \n \n(vii) Disaster Management: Provision of hot cooked meal or Food Security Allowance \nto children when schools are closed d ue to a disaster impacting the State/Un ion \nTerritory, part of State/Union Territory, some S tates/Union Territory  or whole \ncountry as maybe declared by the State/Central G overnment under its powers as \nDisaster Management Authority. \n \n***** \n  \n\n  \n\n ",
    "ls|LS17_S8_U_251": "������������������� �\n��������������������������������������� �\n�\n��������� �\n����������������������� ��� �\n������������ �� � � ��������������� �\n�\n������� ��������� ��� �������� �\n�\n��� ���������������������������������� ��\n� ��������������������������������� ��\n� �\n� ������������������������������������������������� �\n�������������������������������������������������\n��������������������� �\n���� �������� ���� ����������� ��� ������ ����� ���� ������ ��� �������� ����\n� ���������������������������� �\n���� ������� ���� �������� ��� ���������� ��� ���� �������� ��������� ����� �����\n� ������������������������������ �\n���� �������� ���� ����������� ���� ���� ����� ��� �������� ���� �������\n� �������������������������������������������������� �\n���� ��� ���� ���� �������� �������� ������ ����� ���������� ���������� ���� ����\n� ������������ �\n���� ������������������������������������������������������ �\n�\n������ �\n�\n������������������������������������������� �\n�\n��������������������������� �\n�\n���� �������� �\n���� � �������� ��� ��������� ����������� ������ ���� ��� ������� ��� ��������\n� ��������������������������������������������������������� �\n�����������������������������������������������������������������������\n��� ���� ��� ����� ����� ��� ���� ������ ������ ���� ��������� ���� ������� ��\n��������������������������� ����������������������������� ������������\n���� ��������� ���� ����������� ��� ���� ����� ��������� ��� ��������� ���\n����������������������������������������������� �\n����� �\n�",
    "ls|LS17_S8_U_252": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO: 252 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21.03.2022 \n \nImpact of the Ken-Betwa Link Project \n \n252.   SHRI MOHAMMED FAIZAL P.P.: \n \nWill the Minister of ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE be pleased to \nstate: \n \n(a)  whether the Government has assessed the impact of Ken Betwa project on local \npopulation that will be displaced by the project implementation; \n(b) if so, the details thereof, including the number of persons to be displaced and if not, \nreasons therefore;  \n(c) whether the displaced persons will be relocated and provided with any form of \ncompensation; \n(d) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor; and \n(e) the details of the measures taken to ensure that local populations were provided \ndetailed information about the project before being asked to attend public hearings? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER FOR ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE  \n(SHRI BHUPENDER YADAV) \n \n(a) to (e) Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n*** \n \n \n \n \n  \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (e), OF LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 252 FOR ANSWER ON 21/03/2022 RAISED BY SHRI \nMOHAMMED FAIZAL P.P. REGARDING IMPACT OF THE KEN-BETWA LINK \nPROJECT \n \n \nThe Ken-Betwa Link Project is a river link project with the main aim of transferring surplus \nwater of Ken basin to water deficit Betwa basin. The project comprises two phases namely: \n1. Ken-Betwa Link Project Phase – I \n2. Ken–Betwa Link Project, Phase–II \n \nThe Ken-Betwa Link Project Phase – I comprises following components: \ni. Construction of an earth fill and concrete dam near Daudhan village on Ken river.  \nii. Link canal for transferring water from Ken river to Betwa basin.  \n \n \nThe Ken–Betwa Link Project, Phase–II envisages construction of following projects: \n \ni. Lower Orr Dam project at Village Didoni, Tehsil Chanderi, District Ashok \nNagar, Madhya Pradesh  \nii. Kotha Barrage major irrigation project at Village Kotha, Tehsil Kurwai, \nDistrict Vidisha, Madhya Pradesh  \niii. Bina Complex Multipurpose Project (32MW) at Village Chakarpur, Tehsil \nSagar, District Sagar in Madhya Pradesh  \n \nThe Ministry has delineated a detailed procedure for comprehensive assessment of \nenvironmental and social impacts of the project in the Environment Impact Assessment \nNotification, 2006, as amended; which inter-alia provides for preparation of Environment \nImpact Assessment (EIA) Report, Environmental Management Plan (EMP), Disaster \nManagement Plan (DMP) and Public Consultation. The Expert Appraisal Committee (EAC) \ncomprising domain area experts, after detailed examination and deliberation on various \nenvironmental and social aspects of the project, recommend the project for grant of \nEnvironmental Clearance by suggesting suitable mitigation measures and Environment \nManagement to minimize the environmental and social impacts associated with the project.  \n \nThe following projects under Ken- Betwa Link project have been granted Environmental \nClearance (EC) under the provisions of the EIA Notification, 2006 after detailed appraisal by \nthe EAC/SEACconstituted by the Ministry: \ni. Ken-Betwa Link Project Phase-I at Village Daudhan, Tehsil Bijawar, District \nChhatarpurin Madhya Pradesh (EC has been accorded by MoEF&CC on 25.08.2017) \nii. Bina Complex Multipurpose Project (32MW) at Village Chakarpur, Tehsil Sagar, \nDistrict Sagar in Madhya Pradesh (EC has been accorded by MoEF&CC on \n22.02.2017) \niii. Kotha Barrage major irrigation project at Village Kotha, Tehsil Kurwai, District \nVidisha, Madhya Pradesh (EC has been accorded by State Environmental Impact \nAssessment Authority, MP on 21.08.2020). \n \nWhile deliberating on environmental and social issues associated with the above mentioned \nprojects the EAC has observed the following: \n \n\n1. Ken - Betwa Link Project Phase-I \ni. The total land required for Ken-Betwa Link Project Phase-I is about 13236 ha out \nof which the forest land is about 5339 ha. \nii. Panna Tiger Reservefalls within the10 km radius of the project. \niii. 10 villages consisting of 1,585families will be relocated. \niv. Total cost for compensation of properties works out to Rs. 17.64 Crores. \nv. The Economic Rehabilitation and Resettlement package as per the second \nschedule of the Right to Fair Compensation and Transparency in Land \nAcquisition, Rehabilitation and Resettlement Act 2013 has been worked out for \n2667 households (which is inclusive of 1913 PAF head households and 754 \nmajor children) identified under the Social Economic Survey (SES)and is \nestimated at a cost of Rs. 220.14 Crores. The total cost of Resettlement and \nEconomic Rehabilitation is estimated at about Rs. 1,256.25 Crores including cost \nof land acquisition.  \nvi. Total budget allocation for implementation of Environmental Management Plan \nis Rs. 5,064.91 Crores which inter alia includes expenditure related catchment \narea treatment, afforestation, reforestation, erosion control by \nengineering/mechanical measures, development of agricultural lands, muck \ndisposal management, silt management, command area management, habitat \nimprovement in Panna Tiger Reserve, conservation of endangered species, green \nbelt development, Fisheries conservation and development, surface and ground \nwater management, public health management, tourism development, \nResettlement and Rehabilitation etc.  \nvii. Madhya Pradesh Pollution Control Board (MPPCB) published a public notice on \n21st&23rd November, 2014 in local News Paper Dainik Bhaskar and Hindustan \nregarding public hearing. A time period of 30 days was allowed for public to \nrespond.The Public Hearing was conducted on 23rd& 27th December, 2014 in \nChattarpur & Panna Districts respectively as per provisions of the EIA, \nNotification, 2006 as amended. \n \n2. Ken–Betwa Link Project, Phase–II-Bina Complex Multipurpose Project \ni. Total land requirement 12,671.18 ha out of which forest area is 1024.44 ha.  \nii. Total submergence area is 10391.88 ha  \niii. No National Park, Sanctuary, Defense Establishments, Archeological \nMonuments Notified Eco-sensitive areas or protected area under Wildlife \n(Protection) Act exists within the project area or within 10 km distance from it. \niv. 2957 families in 82 villages, of which 34 villages are coming in District Raisen \nand 48 villages are coming under Sagar District, will lose homestead and land \ndue to submergence. \nv. Total budget allocation for implementation of Rehabilitation and Resettlement \n(R&R) Plan is Rs.1609.28 Crores. \nvi. Total budget allocation for implementation of Environmental Management Plan \nis 1735.05 Croreswhich inter alia includes expenditure related to afforestation \nand biodiversity conservation, fisheries management, environment management \nin labour camp, public health delivery system, restoration and landscaping of \nconstruction site, muck management, green belt development, pollution control \nmeasures in air and water, energy conservation measures, public awareness \nprogram, Resettlement and Rehabilitation, catchment area treatment plan, \ndisaster management plan, environmental monitoring etc.  \n\nvii. Madhya Pradesh Pollution Control Board (MPPCB) published a public notice \non 13.04.2016 in local News Paper Dainik Bhaskar and and Nai Duniya in \nRaisen, Vidisha (Patrika and Dainik bhaskar on 13.04.2016) and Sagar \n(DanikBhaskar on 26.04.2016 and Hindustan Times on 27.04.2016) Districts \nregarding public hearing as per provisions of the EIA, Notification, 2006 as \namended. A time period of 30 days was allowed for the public to respond. The \nPublic Hearing was conducted in Raisen District on 18th May, 2016, Vidhisha \nDistrict on 20th May, 2016 and in Sagar District on 27th May, 2016 respectively \nas per provisions of the EIA, Notification, 2006 as amended. \n \n3. Ken–Betwa Link Project, Phase–II-Kotha Barrage Major Irrigation Project \ni. The total land requirement is 1359 ha which is comprised of 678 ha revenue \nland and 681 ha private land respectively. No diversion of forest land required \nfor project. \nii. Total submergence area is 1359 ha. \niii. No National Park, Sanctuary, Defense Establishments, Archeological \nMonuments Notified Eco-sensitive areas or protected area under Wildlife \n(Protection) Act exists within the project area or within 10 km distance from it. \niv. There are 638 project-affected families (Titleholders) whose land would be \nacquired but nofamily would be displaced. Owing to acquisition of land, 27 \nvillages would be partially affected.  \nv. Total budget allocation for implementation of Rehabilitation and Resettlement \n(R&R) Plan isRs. 141.58 Crores. \nvi   Total budget allocation for implementation of Environmental Management Plan \ncost is 169.93 Crores which inter alia includes expenditure related to biodiversity \nconservation, fisheries management, restoration and landscaping, muck \nmanagement, green belt development, pollution control measures in air and \nwater, energy conservation measures, public health delivery,  activates of \nCorporate Environment Responsibility, Resettlement and Rehabilitation, \ncatchment area treatment plan, command area treatment plan, reservoir rim \ntreatment,   disaster management plan, environmental monitoring etc.  \nvii       Madhya Pradesh Pollution Control Board (MPPCB) published a public notice for    \nconducting Public Hearing on 8thJune, 2018 in Hindi Daily, “Dainik Bhaskar”, \n“Nav-Dunia” daily newspaper of Vidisha on 8thJune, 2019. A time period of 30 \ndays was allowed for public to respond. The Public Hearing was conducted on \n10thJuly, 2019 in District Vidisha.  \n \n***** \n \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S8_U_253": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA  \nMINISTRY OF SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP  \n  \nLOK SABHA  \n  \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 253  \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21.03.2022  \n  \nSKILL DEVELOPMENT OF STREET VENDORS  \n  \n†*253.  SHRI GYANESHWAR PATIL:  \n  \nWill the Minister of SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRE NEURSHIP be \npleased to state:  \n  \n(a)        whether any proposals have been submitte d by States for skill development \nof street vendors under the Pradhan Mantri Kaushal Vikas Yojana to help them in \nearning more revenue;  \n  \n(b)        if so, the details thereof;  \n  \n(c)        the details of the employment opportunit ies created for hawkers in the \ncountry including Madhya Pradesh in this regard;  \n  \n(d)        whether the Government proposes to expan d this programme for street \nvendors in Madhya Pradesh; and  \n  \n(e)       if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor?   \n  \nANSWER  \n  \nMINISTER OF  \nSKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP  \n(SHRI DHARMENDRA PRADHAN)  \n  \n(a) to (e)         A Statement is laid on the Table of the House.  \n \n \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (A) TO (E) OF LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 253 TO BE ANSWERED ON 21.03.20 22 ASKED \nBY SHRI GYANESHWAR PATIL REGARDING SKILL DEVELOPMEN T OF \nSTREET VENDORS.  \n  \n(a) to (c)         Under PMKVY, more than 20 thousa nd street food vendors have been \ntrained /oriented through Short -Term Training (STT ) and Recognition of Prior \nLearning (RPL) in 18 States/UTs. Till now, no train ing has been imparted to street \nfood vendors under PMKVY in the State of Madhya Pra desh. The State-wise details, \nas on 31.12.2021, are given at Annexure .  \n  \nMinistry of Skill Development and Entrepreneurship (MSDE) has launched a \npilot project for ‘ Upskilling for Street Food Vendors (for e-cart lice nse)’ in East Delhi \nMunicipal Corporation (EDMC) on 30.11.2021 to train over 2,500 street food vendors \nover a period of four months. The purpose of this s kill development is to impart skills \nwhich would lead to better services to consumers, m ore opportunities to the vendors \nin revenue generation and awareness of regulations,  and adherence to stipulated \nrules and better services for the local bodies. Can didates would be provided with the \ndual certification from the Tourism & Hospitality S kill Council, and the FSSAI. \nDespite disruption caused due to COVID-19 pandemic, 686 street food vendors have \nbeen enrolled and out of which, 417 street food vendors have been oriented till date.  \n  \n(d) and (e) The project of ‘ Upskilling for Street Food Vendors (for e-cart lice nse)’ is \na pilot for East Delhi Municipal Corporation (EDMC) . Further, Ministry has no plan to \nextend this project presently in the State of Madhya Pradesh.  \n  \n\nAnnexure  \n  \nState-wise number of street food vendors benefitted under PMKVY (as on \n31.12.2021)  \n  \nS. \nNo.  States/UTs  Short Term Training (STT)  Recognition of Prior \nLearning (RPL)  \nTrained  Certified  Placed  Oriented  Certified  \n1.  Assam  0 0 0 10,651  7,883  \n2.  Chandigarh  180  165  136  0 0 \n3.  Chhattisgarh  30  19  12  0 0 \n4.  Delhi  279  220  0 996  177  \n5.  Goa  63  54  18  0 0 \n6.  Gujarat  240  187  94  0 0 \n7.  Haryana  0 0 0 100  0 \n8.  Himachal Pradesh  0 0 0 284  118  \n9.  Jharkhand  0 0 0 676  394  \n10.  Maharashtra  56  44  0 0 0 \n11.  Odisha  0 0 0 1,417  983  \n12.  Tamil Nadu  1,006  763  442  0 0 \n13.  Telangana  120  119  0 0 0 \n14.  The Dadra & Nagar \nHaveli and Daman & Diu \n 120  117  87  0 0 \n15.  Tripura  150  144  42  0 0 \n16.  Uttar Pradesh  355  321  271  3,165  2,075  \n17.  Uttarakhand  617  521  299  60  50  \n18.  West Bengal  120  116  2 0 0 \nTotal  3,336  2,790  1,403  17,349  11,680  \n  \n*****  \n  \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S8_U_254": "����������������������������������������\n������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n��\n��������������������\n��������������������������������������������������\n������������������������������������������������������������\n��\n������������������������������������������������������\n��\n���������� ��������������������������������������������\n��\n����������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n��������������\n��\n�������������� ����������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n������������ ���������������� �������� ������������ ���������� ���������� �������������������� �������� ������������������ �������� �������� �������������������������� ������ ������������\n����������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n������������ ����������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n����������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n��\n��\n��������������\n��\n����������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n����������������������������������������������\n��\n������ ����������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n��\n��������\n��\n��\n��\n��\n��\n��\n��\n��\n�� ��\n\n�������������������� ������������������ ������ ������ ������������ ������ �����