 regime for Bangladeshis (from \nPakistanis) facilitating inbound tourism. \n The Bangladesh, Bhutan, India and Nepal (BBIN) Motor Vehicle \nAgreement (MVA) once operationalised will facilita te seamless \nconnectivity with Bangladesh. \n Maitree bridge over river Feni connecting Sabroom (Tripura) with \nRamgarh (Bangladesh), inaugurated in 2021, of 1.9 km length built at \na cost of 133 crores rupees on Feni River connects Tripura and \nBangladesh. \n There are 3 operational bus routes connecting N orth East Region \nwith Bangladesh, passing through Shillong, Guwahati and Agartala. \n(Dhaka-Shillong-Guwahati, Dhaka-Agartala, Kolkata-Dhaka-Agartala). \n\n With the inclusion of Sonamura -Daudkandi route in 2020, there ar e \nnow 10 approved inland waterway routes between India and \nBangladesh for movement of tourists. \n Border infrastructure such as Integrated Check Posts (ICP) and Land \nImmigration Check Posts (ImCPs) facilitate movement of people \nacross border. There are 2 ful ly functional ICPs and 24 LCSs \ncurrently operational in the India-Bangladesh border. \n Currently, seven Border Haats along the India Bangladesh border, \ntwo in Tripura and five in Meghalaya are operational. Three have \nbeen made operational recently at Nalikat a (India) – Saydabad \n(Bangladesh); Ryngku (India) – Bagan Bari (Bangladesh) and Bhola \nGanj. \n To improve connectivity, to promote inbound tourism, rail link \nbetween Agartala (Tripura) and Akhaura (Bangladesh) is being \ndeveloped.  \n The Indo -Myanmar-Thailand Tr ilateral Highway, linking Moreh in \nManipur to Mae Sot in Thailand, running through the length of \nMyanmar to enable seamless flow of inbound tourism is being \ndeveloped.  \n Free Movement Regime within 16 kms of border is facilitated, to \nenable people from neig hbouring countries to enhance ease of visit \nand shop in India, with due safeguards within the parameters of the \nRestricted Area Permit. \n Resident Ambassadors of neighbouring countries were taken to \nfestivals in border areas like Nagaland to showcase the eth nic \naffinity and rich cultural heritage of the North-East. \n In December 2019, a Legislative delegation from Sagaing and \nMandalay visited Imphal, where they met with local tourism agencies \nand exchanged ideas on promoting tourism. \n Indian Chamber of Internati onal Business (lClB) in association with \nMission and India -Myanmar Chamber of Commerce organized virtual \ntrade, tourism and investment related event titled 'Look East Act \nEast - Opportunities in North East India and neighbours, Myanmar, \nBhutan and Bangladesh\" on 21 January 2021. \n \n********* \n ",
